    "case_id": "527",
    "case_summary": "A 34-year-old Thai man with a history of hypertension and tobacco use disorder, and a family history significant for polycythemia, hypertension, and stroke in his father. He initially presented with worsening bifrontal headaches, nausea, vomiting, and right-sided blurry vision. He was not adherent to his antihypertensive regimen. Three months later, he presented again with right-sided frontal headache, photophobia, tingling in his right fingers, nausea, and vomiting. He reported missing a few doses of his carvedilol and had cut down on his smoking.\n\nBlood pressures in the emergency department ranged from 180 to 190/80-90 mm Hg during initial presentation. On second presentation, systolic blood pressures were 150 to 170 mm Hg. National Institutes of Health Stroke Scale score was 2 because of complete left homonymous hemianopsia.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Initial presentation showed hemoglobin 20.2 g/dL, hematocrit 54.8%, MCV 74 fL, and thrombocytopenia (91,000/mm³). Second presentation showed hemoglobin 20.1 g/dL, hematocrit 57.2%, MCV 76 fL, and thrombocytopenia (76,000/mm³). Erythrocyte sedimentation rate was elevated (21 mm/h). Erythropoietin was normal (7.8 mIU/mL).- Imaging studies:  - CT Head: Left occipital intraparenchymal hemorrhage with intravascular extension (first presentation)  - CT Head: Right posterior parietal intraparenchymal hemorrhage (4.7 × 2.9 × 4.2 cm) with blood product in the right lateral ventricle and minimal midline shift (second presentation)  - MRI Brain: No underlying masses  - Peripheral smear: Showed microcytosis and thrombocytopenia without toxic granulations or blast cells- Hemoglobin Electrophoresis Results:  Hb A: 0.0%  Hb F: 1.7%  Hb A2: 4.4%  Hb E: 34.0%  Hb Tak: 59.9%",
